WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.855
Memory safety is central to IoT device
security because many devices run C code with

2
00:00:04.935 --> 00:00:09.165
constrained RAM, fixed buffers, small task
stacks, and direct hardware access.

3
00:00:09.245 --> 00:00:13.413
Memory behavior should be predictable
under normal operation, unusual input,

4
00:00:13.493 --> 00:00:17.351
network timeouts, reconnects, reset
sequences, and field diagnostics.

5
00:00:17.431 --> 00:00:19.795
Fixed buffers require strict bounds
checks.

6
00:00:19.875 --> 00:00:23.483
A message buffer, command buffer, sensor
frame, certificate field,

7
00:00:23.563 --> 00:00:27.420
endpoint string, or formatted log line
should be checked before copying,

8
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.429
appending, parsing, or converting.

9
00:00:29.509 --> 00:00:33.366
Buffer capacity should be tied to a
product limit that reviewers can find,

10
00:00:33.446 --> 00:00:35.747
not to a value inherited from an old
example.

11
00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:38.440
Stack and heap policy should match device
limits.

12
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.382
Some devices avoid dynamic allocation
after startup.

13
00:00:41.462 --> 00:00:44.137
Others use controlled allocation or memory
pools.

14
00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:47.203
Whatever the policy, it should define
failure behavior,

15
00:00:47.283 --> 00:00:49.647
cleanup responsibility, object ownership,

16
00:00:49.727 --> 00:00:53.460
and whether allocation is allowed during
startup, update, reconnect,

17
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:54.660
or command handling.

18
00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.228
Parser state should remain small and
explicit.

19
00:00:57.308 --> 00:01:01.539
Network frames, local commands,
configuration records, mobile-app messages,

20
00:01:01.619 --> 00:01:05.787
and provisioning data should be processed
with maximum lengths, known states,

21
00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:07.485
and clear rejection behavior.

22
00:01:07.565 --> 00:01:11.733
A parser should not accumulate unbounded
data while waiting for a delimiter or a

23
00:01:11.813 --> 00:01:12.497
later field.

24
00:01:12.577 --> 00:01:14.879
Integer sizes matter in protocol handling.

25
00:01:14.959 --> 00:01:18.318
Length fields, counters, offsets,
timeouts, version numbers,

26
00:01:18.398 --> 00:01:20.887
and register values often use specific
widths.

27
00:01:20.967 --> 00:01:24.948
Reviewers should look for signedness
confusion, truncation, wraparound,

28
00:01:25.028 --> 00:01:29.010
and conversions that cause a check to mean
something different from what the

29
00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.210
developer intended.

30
00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.716
Object ownership prevents stale
references.

31
00:01:32.796 --> 00:01:35.471
If a buffer moves from a driver to
application code,

32
00:01:35.551 --> 00:01:37.728
from a network layer to a command handler,

33
00:01:37.808 --> 00:01:39.861
or from a provisioning flow to storage,

34
00:01:39.941 --> 00:01:42.928
the code should make clear who owns it and
when it is valid.

35
00:01:43.008 --> 00:01:46.989
Safe rejection should clean up partial
state and keep later input from being

36
00:01:47.069 --> 00:01:48.500
interpreted incorrectly.
